Milestone: Two wall shelves, six game boxes and local click-to-play library.
Dependencies: 14, 80, 111.
Purpose: Replace the decorative loose cartridges with three GBA and three DS
dummy boxes on separate shelves. Keep the archived layout recoverable and
make the physical library usable without repeatedly locating a ROM.

Tasks:
- [x] Build original cardboard GBA boxes and hinged plastic DS cases.
- [x] Stack three of each spine-out on separate labelled wall shelves.
- [x] Archive the old loose-cartridge arrangement without deleting its factory.
- [x] Animate cover presentation, opening, extraction and console insertion.
- [x] Connect validated local ROMs to the existing console/save lifecycle.
- [x] Keep each console's last cartridge on the desk; replay A -> B -> A,
      not same-current selection; restore cached cartridges without unpacking.
- [x] Define precise local ROM/cover folders and browser import alternatives.
- [x] Qualify rebuilt desktop/mobile models, animation and selection behavior.
- [x] Update focused documentation, development ledger and owned continuity.

Acceptance criteria:
Exactly three distinguishable GBA boxes and three DS cases with original dummy
art and readable title spines are stacked horizontally on two wall shelves.
First/different selection shows the complete appropriate opening sequence.
Already-current selection retains the existing session without unpacking.
Returning A -> B -> A replays it. Each console's successfully selected library
cartridge has one stable casually placed desk prop participating in clearance.
Browser imports stay origin-local and use existing ROM validation/save paths.
Optional folder serving stays loopback-only; the normal static build excludes
all private ROMs and covers. Dummy previews explicitly have no ROM.
The old decorative layout remains recoverable. No commercial example art is
copied. User titles/ROMs are not invented. No book rebuild/commit/push required.

Completion evidence:
- Final npm run check and npm run build PASS.
